SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}Thin the reference record to a 1-in-6 or 1-in-12 schedule and most PM$_{2.5}$ equivalence verdicts stop being determinate: across 43,576 same-site FRM--FEM pairs at 45 US sites, sparse-schedule indeterminacy reached 63.8\,\% (53.0--73.8\,\%) and 73.8\,\% (63.7--82.6\,\%), and 64.6\,\% of near-daily benchmarks were already boundary-uncertain.}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
Yangtze University, \textit{Measurement Science and Technology}; 127 episodes (2022--2025), US slope--intercept equivalence envelope for the point verdict, circular moving-block bootstrap for its stability. At 1-in-3 indeterminacy was 33.0\,\% (21.1--46.1\,\%); 88 of 90 point mismatches became indeterminate rather than reversed. In a known-truth 1-in-12 simulation interval coverage was about 88\,\% and 14 of 64 ``stable'' verdicts had the wrong sign.
\textbf{Where it is weak:} one group, statistical rather than field work, and the stability rule (0.95/0.05) is a choice --- the authors show the ordering holds from 0.80 to 0.99, not the levels.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} low-cost sensor ``equivalence'' and drift-correction claims routinely rest on fewer reference days than a 1-in-6 schedule. A point verdict without its bootstrap state and reference support is not evidence of comparability."""

